    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Born in Russian-ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, Maria Skłodowska studied at the clandestine Flying University, then in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> from 1891, where she married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-curie">Pierre Curie</a> in 1895 and with him announced polonium and radium in 1898.</li><li>She won the 1903 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Physics with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-curie">Pierre Curie</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-becquerel">Henri Becquerel</a> and the 1911 Nobel Prize in Chemistry alone, the first person to win a Nobel Prize twice and the first woman to win one.</li><li>She met the barriers of her sex and her foreign birth, was vilified by the right-wing press in 1911, and died in 1934 of aplastic anaemia believed to have come from long exposure to radiation.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Curies did not patent the process for isolating radium, and Marie Curie insisted that monetary gifts and awards go to the scientific institutions she was affiliated with rather than to her; her papers from the 1890s are still too radioactive to handle without protective clothing.</p></aside>
    </section>

