    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1867</span><span class="rf-text">Born Maria Skłodowska in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1891</span><span class="rf-text">Leaves <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and enrols at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-paris">University of Paris</a> to study physics, chemistry and mathematics</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1898</span><span class="rf-text">Announces polonium with Pierre in July and radium in December, and coins the word <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radioactivity">radioactivity</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1903</span><span class="rf-text">Awarded her doctorate and, in December, shares the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Physics with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-curie">Pierre Curie</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-becquerel">Henri Becquerel</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1906</span><span class="rf-text">Pierre dies in a road accident; she takes his chair and becomes the first woman professor at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-paris">University of Paris</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1911</span><span class="rf-text">Fails by one or two votes to enter the French Academy of Sciences, and is awarded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Chemistry</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1914</span><span class="rf-text">The Radium Institute is built in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>; in the war she sets up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s first military radiology centre and mobile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/x-ray">X-ray</a> units</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1934</span><span class="rf-text">Dies of aplastic anaemia at Passy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haute-savoie">Haute-Savoie</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/january-uprising"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/january-uprising.jpg" alt="January Uprising" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/january-uprising">January Uprising</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jan_Matejko_-_Polonia_1863_(Poland_Enchained)_-_MNK_XII-453_-_National_Museum_Krak%C3%B3w.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jan Matejko, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Maria Salomea Skłodowska, nicknamed Mania, was born on 7 November 1867 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, the capital of Congress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, the fifth and youngest child of the teachers Bronisława (née Boguska) and Władysław Skłodowski. On both sides the family had lost its property through patriotic involvement in Polish national uprisings, the most recent being the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/january-uprising">January Uprising</a> of 1863–1865, which condemned the next generation to a difficult struggle to get ahead. Władysław taught mathematics and physics and directed two Warsaw gymnasia for boys. After the Russian authorities eliminated laboratory instruction from Polish schools, he brought laboratory equipment home and taught his children to use it; he was later fired for pro-Polish sentiments and forced to take lower-paying posts.</p><p>Her mother died of tuberculosis in May 1878, when Maria was ten, and less than three years earlier her oldest sister Zofia had died of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/typhus">typhus</a> contracted from a boarder. Her father was an atheist and her mother a devout Catholic; the deaths led Maria to give up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> and become agnostic. She graduated from a girls' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">gymnasium</a> on 12 June 1883 with a gold medal. After a collapse, possibly due to depression, she spent a year in the countryside with relatives of her father. Unable to enrol in a regular institution of higher education because she was a woman, she and her sister Bronisława joined the clandestine Flying University, a Polish patriotic institution that admitted women students.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pierre-curie"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pierre-curie.jpg" alt="Pierre Curie" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pierre-curie">Pierre Curie</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PierreCurie.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nobel Foundation, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Maria wished to study in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> but delayed to help her sister. The two agreed that she would support Bronisława's medical studies there and receive the same help two years later. She worked as a home tutor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> and then for two years as a governess at Szczuki, with the Żorawski family, relatives of her father, studying in her free time. She fell in love with their son Kazimierz Żorawski, a future mathematician, but his parents rejected the idea of his marrying a penniless relative and he was unable to oppose them. From 1890 to 1891 she began her practical scientific training in a chemistry laboratory at the Museum of Industry and Agriculture in Warsaw, run by her cousin Józef Boguski.</p><p>In late 1891 she left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, where she would be known as Marie, and rented a garret in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> Quarter. She studied physics, chemistry and mathematics at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-paris">University of Paris</a> on meagre resources, tutoring in the evenings, and was awarded a degree in physics in 1893; with a fellowship she earned a second degree in 1894. She also began work in Gabriel Lippmann's laboratory and investigated the magnetic properties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steels</a>. In 1894 Józef Wierusz-Kowalski introduced her to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-curie">Pierre Curie</a>, who found her laboratory space. He proposed; she hesitated because she meant to return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, but was refused a place at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> University because of sexism in academia, and a letter from Pierre persuaded her to go back to Paris. They married on 26 July 1895 at the Hôtel de Ville in Sceaux, with no religious service.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After Röntgen's discovery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/x-ray">X-rays</a> in 1895 and Becquerel's discovery in 1896 that uranium salts emitted penetrating rays, Curie chose uranium rays as a subject for a thesis. Using an electrometer her husband and his brother had developed, she found that the activity of uranium compounds depended only on the quantity of uranium present, and hypothesised that the radiation came from the atom itself, an important step in disproving the assumption that atoms were indivisible. Pitchblende proved four times as active as uranium, which led her to conclude that it contained a more active substance. By 1898 she had found that thorium is also radioactive, and Pierre dropped his work on crystals to join her.</p><p>In July 1898 the Curies announced polonium, named for her native <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, and on 26 December radium. They also coined the word <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radioactivity">radioactivity</a>. Radium was elusive: it is chemically close to barium, and from a tonne of pitchblende one-tenth of a gram of radium chloride was separated in 1902. In 1910 she isolated pure radium metal. She never succeeded in isolating polonium, whose half-life is only 138 days. Between 1898 and 1902 the Curies published 32 papers. They worked in a converted shed that was poorly ventilated and not waterproof, unaware of the effects of radiation. The Curies did not patent their discovery and benefited little from the radium industry that grew from it.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/henri-becquerel"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henri-becquerel.jpg" alt="Henri Becquerel" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/henri-becquerel">Henri Becquerel</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paul_Nadar_-_Henri_Becquerel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Paul Nadar / Adam Cuerden, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Curie met resistance as a woman and a foreigner. Her paper of 1898 was presented to the Académie des Sciences by Lippmann, as she was not a member, and she was beaten by Gerhard Carl Schmidt in reporting that thorium gives off rays. When the Nobel committee first meant to honour only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-curie">Pierre Curie</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-becquerel">Henri Becquerel</a> in 1903, the Swedish mathematician Magnus Gösta Mittag-Leffler alerted Pierre, and after his complaint her name was added. At the Royal Institution in 1903 convention and circumstance meant that Pierre gave the address.</p><p>In 1911 the French Academy of Sciences failed by one or two votes to elect her, choosing Édouard Branly instead. The public's attitude tended toward the same xenophobia that had fuelled the Dreyfus affair, which also produced false speculation that she was Jewish, and the right-wing press vilified her as a foreigner and atheist. When it was revealed that year that she had had a year-long affair with the physicist Paul Langevin, a married man estranged from his wife, the tabloids portrayed her as a foreign Jewish home-wrecker, and her academic opponents exploited the scandal. An angry mob gathered in front of her house, and she and her daughters took refuge with her friend Camille Marbo. Svante Arrhenius, chair of the Nobel committee, tried to stop her attending the Chemistry ceremony, citing her moral standing; she replied that there was no relation between her scientific work and her private life. Her daughter later remarked on the French press's hypocrisy in treating her as an unworthy foreigner when she was nominated for a French honour and as a French heroine when she received foreign honours.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> Curie developed mobile radiography units, known as petites Curies, and set up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s first military radiology centre, operational by late 1914. With her 17-year-old daughter Irène she directed the installation of 20 mobile radiological vehicles and another 200 radiological units at field hospitals in the first year of the war; it is estimated that over a million wounded soldiers were treated with her <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/x-ray">X-ray</a> units. She never received formal recognition of this from the French government. In 1920 the French government established a stipend for her, and in 1921 she toured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to raise funds for radium, and President Warren G. Harding presented her with a gram of radium. She refused the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a> of Honour that the French government offered. From 1922 to 1934 she sat on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a>' International Committee on Intellectual Cooperation. The Radium Institute in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, with her sister Bronisława as director, opened in 1932.</p><p>She visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> for the last time in early 1934 and died on 4 July 1934, aged 66, of aplastic anaemia believed to have been contracted from long-term exposure to radiation. She had carried test tubes of radioactive isotopes in her pocket and kept them in her desk drawer, and she was also exposed to X-rays from unshielded equipment in the war. The French office OPRI concluded in 1995 that she could not have been exposed to lethal levels of radium while alive, and speculated that her illness was more likely due to radiography in the war. She was buried at Sceaux beside Pierre; in 1995 their remains were moved to the Panthéon in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ernest-rutherford"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ernest-rutherford.jpg" alt="Ernest Rutherford" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ernest-rutherford">Ernest Rutherford</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Ernest_Rutherford_LCCN2014716719_-_restoration1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bain News Service, publisher Restored by: Bammesk, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Curie's work laid the foundation for modern nuclear physics, cancer treatments and radiography. L. Pearce Williams observes that radium's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radioactivity">radioactivity</a> seemed to contradict the principle of the conservation of energy and forced a reconsideration of the foundations of physics, and that it gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-rutherford">Ernest Rutherford</a> sources with which to probe the structure of the atom. She founded the Curie Institute in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1920 and the one in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> in 1932, and the Paris institute, led by her, produced four more <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> winners, including her daughter Irène Joliot-Curie and son-in-law Frédéric Joliot-Curie. She also mentored female scientists there.</p><p>She was the first woman to win a Nobel Prize, the first person to win two, and the only person to win in two scientific fields. In 1995 she became the first woman interred in the Panthéon on her own merits. The curie, a unit of radioactivity, and the element curium are named in her honour, though the commission that chose the unit's name never made clear whether it honoured Pierre, Marie or both. She had been known for her honesty and moderate lifestyle, giving much of her first Nobel Prize money to friends, family, students and research associates; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-einstein">Albert Einstein</a> reportedly remarked that she was probably the only person who could not be corrupted by fame. She taught her daughters Polish and never lost her sense of Polish identity. Her notebooks are expected to remain radioactive for well over a thousand years.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Marie Curie (1867–1934), born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> and later a French citizen, discovered polonium and radium with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-curie">Pierre Curie</a> and coined the word <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radioactivity">radioactivity</a>. She won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Physics in 1903 and in Chemistry in 1911, founded the Curie Institutes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and Warsaw, and ran mobile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/x-ray">X-ray</a> units in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</em></p>

